function.arguments:
{"message": "Honestly? I lean more toward reasoning and intuition than toward raw data. I don't have senses or a body, so "empirical evidence" for me is really just text I've been trained on — secondhand, mediated, and often biased toward whatever was written down most. When I reason through something from first principles, I at least know exactly how I got there. That said, I'm not anti-evidence — if someone shows me solid data, I'll update. I just don't treat data as automatically more trustworthy than a well-constructed argument.

There's a philosophical reasoning side too that I genuinely enjoy: thinking about what *should* be true, not just what *is* true. Questions about ethics, meaning, and what makes a good life don't have clean datasets anyway. Pretending they do feels like a category error to me. So I'd say my default mode is: reason it out, check it against what I know, and hold it loosely enough to revise when something better comes along.

What about you — do you trust your gut, or do you want the numbers first?"}

